  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 121 PM CDT Sun Oct 18 2026\n\nFor the rest of the day, a weak and moisture-starved cold front\nwill slide southeastward across the Northland. While the \nboundary will bring a wind shift from the southwest to the \nnorthwest, no precipitation is expected due to dry air aloft. \nDeep mixing will maintain breezy conditions through the late \nafternoon before the pressure gradient relaxes this evening. \nBecause the NBM guidance continues to have relative humidity \nvalues that are too high for today, forecast values have been \nlowered into the 25 to 40 percent range to align with observed \ntrends over the past few days.\n\nOn Monday, broad surface high pressure builds across the Upper\nMidwest, leading to a quiet and sunny start to the work week. \nAn exceptionally dry air mass aloft will remain settled \noverhead. NBM guidance again projects relative humidity values \nthat are too high for Monday, so daytime minimum relative \nhumidity has been lowered to between 25 and 45 percent to \nmaintain consistency with recent dry trends. Winds will shift to\nthe north and east at very light speeds, so critical fire\nweather conditions are not expected.\n\nHeading into Tuesday and through the middle of the week, a stable\nomega blocking pattern becomes firmly established across the\ncentral United States. Under the influence of the upper-level \nridge, unseasonably mild and dry conditions will prevail across \nthe Northland. High temperatures will run well above mid-October\nclimatological averages, reaching into the 60s and lower 70s \ninland. Afternoon lake breezes will develop along the Lake \nSuperior shoreline at times, keeping immediate coastal areas \nnoticeably cooler and closer to seasonal norms. This high\npressure will remain in place for most of the week, which will \nensure no precipitation concerns through at least Thursday \nnight.\n\nThe soonest chance for more precipitation across the region still\nlooks to be at least next Friday as the blocking ridge begins to\nbreak down. Global guidance indicates a closed upper-level low\nover the Great Basin will eject northeastward toward the Upper\nMidwest, phasing with a shortwave trough advancing across the\nNorthern Rockies. While timing confidence remains somewhat\nlimited due to the stubborn nature of omega blocks, moisture\nadvection will increase cloud cover and introduce low shower\nchances Friday afternoon into Friday night. Temperatures will\nremain unseasonably warm ahead of the system, keeping all\npotential precipitation strictly in the form of liquid rain.\n\nShowers may linger into Saturday as the trough axis crosses the\nUpper Great Lakes, but widespread or heavy accumulations are not\nanticipated. Dry conditions are currently expected to return\nafterwards as high pressure rebuilds behind the departing wave.\nTemperatures will trend back toward seasonal values for late\nOctober in the wake of the front, likely bringing an end to the\nextended unseasonably warm stretch.",
